$DIR/main.p:6:1: //pu:resultholds is for the standard library; hold cannot use it
    //pu:resultholds is a promise the compiler cannot check: that a function's
    result gives a parameter back when it is dropped, as alloc.Use's Restore
    gives back the allocator it installed. The standard library uses it for the
    few functions that need it. A program's own code does not need it: the
    compiler works out by itself what a function keeps.
    Remove the directive. If the compiler then rejects a call because the
    function keeps an argument, pass memory that lives long enough, such as an
    allocated value or a package variable.
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3008" for a longer explanation)
